window.docContents[44].resolve({"/Elementary-Functions/___4___39-Continued-Fractions/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___39-Continued-Fractions--___4___39-Continued-Fractions--Hyperbolic-tangent":{"contents":"The pole set and the finite bottom-up construction are explicit in Lean.  At\ndepth n, tanhTail 0 n z ends at the denominator 2n+3, so the convergent\ncontains exactly the first n+1 odd denominators after the leading 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe DLMF continued fraction is\n\\operatorname{tanh}z=\\frac{z}{1+\\frac{z^2}{3+\\frac{z^2}{5+\\frac{z^2}{7+\\cdots}}}}$$\nfor complex z away from the poles\nz = ±(2m+1)π i/2.  The nth Lean convergent retains the denominator 1\nand then the first n+1 odd denominators beginning with 3; its limit is\nthe canonical complex hyperbolic tangent (A&S 4.5.70).\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Elementary Functions\u0009§4.39 Continued Fractions\u0009§4.39 Continued Fractions","header":"Hyperbolic tangent","id":"/Elementary-Functions/___4___39-Continued-Fractions/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___39-Continued-Fractions--___4___39-Continued-Fractions--Hyperbolic-tangent"},"/Exponential___-Logarithmic___-Sine___-and-Cosine-Integrals/___6___12-Asymptotic-Expansions/#chapter-6-section-12":{"contents":"Source: NIST Digital Library of Mathematical Functions, §6.12, Asymptotic Expansions.\n\n\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Exponential, Logarithmic, Sine, and Cosine Integrals","header":"6.12. §6.12 Asymptotic Expansions","id":"/Exponential___-Logarithmic___-Sine___-and-Cosine-Integrals/___6___12-Asymptotic-Expansions/#chapter-6-section-12"},"/Exponential___-Logarithmic___-Sine___-and-Cosine-Integrals/___6___20-Approximations/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Exponential___-Logarithmic___-Sine___-and-Cosine-Integrals--___6___20-Approximations--___6___20-Approximations--6___20_LPAR_ii_RPAR_-Expansions-in-Chebyshev-Series":{"contents":"Clenshaw (1962) gives Chebyshev coefficients for -E_1(x)-\\ln|x| on\n-4\\leq x\\leq 4 and for e^xE_1(x) on x\\geq 4, with 20D accuracy.\nLuke and Wimp (1963) cover \\operatorname{Ei}(x) for x\\leq -4 (20D),\nand \\operatorname{Si}(x) and \\operatorname{Ci}(x) for x\\geq 4 (20D).\n\nLuke (1969b, pp. 41–42) gives Chebyshev expansions of\n\\operatorname{Ein}(ax), \\operatorname{Si}(ax), and\n\\operatorname{Cin}(ax) for -1\\leq x\\leq 1, with a\\in\\mathbb{C}; the\ncoefficients are expressed through series of Bessel functions. On pp. 321–322\nthe same source covers \\operatorname{Ein}(x) and\n-\\operatorname{Ein}(-x) for 0\\leq x\\leq 8 (20D), E_1(x) for x\\geq 5\n(20D), and \\operatorname{Ei}(x) for x\\geq 8 (15D). Its sine- and\ncosine-integral coefficients are given on pp. 325–327.\n\nLuke (1969b, p. 25) gives a Chebyshev expansion near infinity for the\nconfluent hypergeometric function U (see DLMF §13.2(i)).\nUsing DLMF 6.11.2 and\nDLMF 6.11.3, expansions near infinity for\nE_1(z), f(z), and g(z) follow. Luke supplies a recursion for the\ncoefficients of the U expansions; when |\\operatorname{ph}z|<\\pi, it can\nbe used backward.\n\nThe list records the five Chebyshev-expansion source families named by DLMF.\nThe domains, digit accuracies, Bessel-series description, and backward-recursion\ncondition are stated in the surrounding prose above.\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Exponential, Logarithmic, Sine, and Cosine Integrals\u0009§6.20 Approximations\u0009§6.20 Approximations","header":"6.20(ii) Expansions in Chebyshev Series","id":"/Exponential___-Logarithmic___-Sine___-and-Cosine-Integrals/___6___20-Approximations/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Exponential___-Logarithmic___-Sine___-and-Cosine-Integrals--___6___20-Approximations--___6___20-Approximations--6___20_LPAR_ii_RPAR_-Expansions-in-Chebyshev-Series"}});